<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">7.8 Section Exercises</heading>
    <heading level="1">Verbal</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Explain why we can always evaluate the determinant of a square matrix.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>A determinant is the sum and products of the entries in the matrix, so you can always evaluate that product—even if it does end up being 0.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Examining Cramer’s Rule, explain why there is no unique solution to the system when the determinant of your matrix is 0. For simplicity, use a $$2 \times 2$$ matrix.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="3" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Explain what it means in terms of an inverse for a matrix to have a 0 determinant.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>The inverse does not exist.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="4" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The determinant of $$2 \times 2$$ matrix $$A$$ is 3. If you switch the rows and multiply the first row by 6 and the second row by 2, explain how to find the determinant and provide the answer.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
